llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): on gfx803, prompt
batches of quantized matmuls run as fp32 GEMMs on the image's gfx803 SGEMM,
with the weights dequantized into padded rows.

WHY
---
Prompt processing goes through mmq, which emulates int8 dot products: gfx803
has no dp4a, and q4_K at 512 tokens (4096x14336) runs at about 2.4 effective
TOPS, a third of the card's fp32 FMA rate. The base image preloads a gfx803
SGEMM that reaches 3.6-4.5 TFLOPS on these shapes, so dequantizing a weight
to fp32 and multiplying it there is faster, and more precise, since mmq also
quantizes the activations to 8 bits.

Three things stood in the way:
1. For quantized weights, ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas picks fp16 compute on
   every AMD GPU. On gfx803 that is cuBLAS's fp16 path with fp16
   accumulation, which goes to rocBLAS rather than the SGEMM.
2. A weight row of 4096 or 14336 floats is a multiple of 8 KiB. That stride
   puts every row of a GEMM tile in one DRAM channel: 14336 x 512 x 4096
   runs at 2.8 TFLOPS with contiguous rows and at 4.1 with 16 floats of
   padding per row. The same holds for the activations' rows (src1).
3. mmq is chosen for every quantized type before cuBLAS is considered.

Measured on an RX 590 with the base image's SGEMM, Qwen3 8B Q4_K_M prompt
tok/s by ubatch, cuBLAS fp32 vs mmq:
  16: 36 vs 100, 64: 132 vs 146, 96: 130 vs 149, 128: 178 vs 154,
  256: 210 vs 160, 512: 222 vs 162.
Ollama, ~5.2k-token prompt: Qwen3 8B 149 -> 215 tok/s, Qwen2.5-Coder 7B
107 -> 136 tok/s, generation unchanged. KL divergence against the CPU
backend (llama-perplexity, 8 x 512 tokens): Qwen2.5-Coder 7B 0.00152 (mmq)
-> 0.00094, same top token 97.6% -> 98.0%; Qwen3 8B 0.0103 -> 0.0086.
llama-bench, q8_0 KV cache, flash attention, mmq -> this patch:
  Qwen3 8B Q4_K_M         pp512 163.5 -> 222.8, at 8192 deep 127.6 -> 158.8,
                          pp12288 135.0 -> 172.7
  Qwen2.5-Coder 7B Q4_K_M pp512 166.9 -> 230.5, at 8192 deep 57.2 -> 64.1
Dequantizing into a contiguous buffer and copying it to padded rows instead
reached 203.5 for Qwen3 8B pp512. Padding only the weight reached 196.8.
test-backend-ops MUL_MAT 1301/1301 against the CPU backend, both with this
threshold and with the cuBLAS route forced for every batch above 8.
Generation is unchanged: single-token and small batches still use mmvq.

Matmuls whose expert weights are selected per token (MUL_MAT_ID, MoE models)
are not routed here and keep mmq. Neither are weights whose fp32 copy would
exceed 512 MiB: on an 8 GB card that copy competes with the model for VRAM.
The largest feed-forward weights take ~270 MB (Qwen2.5 7B), while an output
head over a 152k vocabulary, which reaches this path only when logits are
computed for every token (llama-perplexity), would need 2.5 GB. Without the
cap that perplexity run (Qwen3 8B, -c 512, batch 2048) ended in a GPU memory
fault ("Page not present", a write) on the stock HSA runtime; with it the run
completes and its KL divergence against the CPU backend is 0.0086 (mmq:
0.0103). The SGEMM itself was run alone at that shape (151936 x 2048 x 4096)
and returned results identical to its reference kernel without a fault, so
the fault comes from the memory pressure, not from the GEMM.

WHAT
----
1. convert.cu/.cuh: ggml_get_to_fp32_ld_cuda(type, ne00) returns a
   dequantizer that writes rows ld floats apart: one generic kernel for
   q4_0, q4_1, q5_0, q5_1 and q8_0, and one per QK_K super-block for the
   K-quants and IQ types (nullptr when QK_K does not divide ne00).
2. ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas: quantized weights on GCN4 use fp32 compute.
3. ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas_impl, fp32 on GCN4 with a single contiguous
   weight matrix: dequantize into rows of ne00 + 16 floats and give src1 a
   copy with rows of ne10 + 16 floats.
4. ggml_cuda_mul_mat: on GCN4, from 128 src1 columns on, matmuls with such a
   dequantizer and an fp32 copy of at most 512 MiB go to
   ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas before mmq is considered.

